(a) There is hereby established a "restaurant district" within the city wherein open containers of alcoholic beverages shall be permitted as provided herein.
(b) The restaurant district shall consist of the area described below and as illustrated on the "restaurant district map" below:
(1) All sidewalks along the side of the buildings and public parking lots that front North and South Jefferson Street on the western side of the street from the intersection of North Jefferson and West Gaines Street on the north to the intersection of South Jefferson and the first set of Seaboard Coastline's railroad tracks on the south;
(2) All sidewalks along the side of the buildings and public parking lots that front South Church Street on the eastern side of the street from the northernmost corner on the east of Bellevue Avenue and South Church Street on the north to the first set of Seaboard and Coastline's railroad tracks on the south;
(3) All sidewalks that front Bellevue Avenue and West Jackson Street on the north side of the street from North Church Street to Jefferson Street;
(4) All sidewalks that front Roosevelt Street on either side of the road from W. Gaines Street on the north to the first set of Seaboard Coastline's railroad tracks on the south;
(5) The sidewalk that fronts West Gaines Street on the south side of the street only from Roosevelt Street on the west to North Jefferson Street on the east;
(6) The sidewalks that front Monroe Street on either side of the street from Bellevue Avenue on the north to the first set of Seaboard and Coastline's railroad tracks on the south;
(7) All associated pedestrian cross walks, and any other public spaces, including the Jackson Street Plaza and Bicentennial Plaza, other than lanes of travel for traffic in the street, within the boundaries created by subsections (1) through (6) above and as depicted on the restaurant district map below;
(8) Restaurant District Map:
(c) The following regulations shall apply to this section:
(1) Any establishment licensed to dispense alcoholic beverages by the drink for consumption on the premises is authorized to dispense alcoholic beverages in a clear plastic cup for removal from the premises, provided, however, that no establishment shall dispense to any person more than one (1) such alcoholic beverage at a time for removal from the premises.
(2) In accordance with section 14-5 of this Code of Ordinances, it shall be unlawful for any person to remove an open container of alcoholic beverage from the restaurant district as described in this section.
(3) No container in which an alcoholic beverage is dispensed and removed from the licensed premises shall exceed sixteen (16) fluid ounces in size. No person shall hold in possession on the streets and sidewalks, in parks and squares, or in other public places within the restaurant district any open alcoholic beverage container containing alcohol which exceeds sixteen (16) fluid ounces in size or which is not in a clear plastic cup issued by a business authorized to sell alcohol by the drink for consumption on the premises.
(4) It shall be unlawful for any person to drink or attempt to drink any alcoholic beverage from a can, bottle, or glass or to possess in an open can, bottle, or glass any alcoholic beverages on the streets, sidewalks, rights-of-way, and parking lots, whether public or private.
(5) Consumption of alcoholic beverages described in this section shall be limited to the hours of 11:00 a.m. through 10:00 p.m. on Mondays through Saturdays and from 12:30 p.m. until 10:00 p.m. on Sundays, unless approved by the city manager for a special event pursuant to section 4-29.
(6) Subsection (a) above does not apply to schools, churches, daycare facilities, or anywhere private property owners or tenants do not allow it. A business may decline to allow an open container of alcoholic beverage on the premises by posting a sign that states "Outside Drinks Not Allowed" in a conspicuous place on their property.
(7) It shall be unlawful for any person to drink or attempt to drink or to transport or attempt to transport any alcoholic beverages in an open container in any part of a motor vehicle within the restaurant district. This section does not limit the enforcement of section 14-5 or any state law regulating the possession of alcoholic beverages within motor vehicles.
(8) No person under the age of twenty-one (21) may consume of any alcoholic beverage at any time under this section.
(d) The provisions of this section provide an exception to section 14-5 of this Code of Ordinances, which shall otherwise remain in full force and effect throughout the city.
(e) Any person violating any of the requirements of this section shall be guilty of a misdemeanor, punishable by a fine up to one thousand dollars ($1,000.00) and/or twelve (12) months in jail.
